      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178562/assets/27763c2083cbfc9961a619fb8e4f9bbb50791f446b734aef2a4f822e5d04de4b.png)\n\nDetermine the slope of the line shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "The line is horizontal, and therefore it has a slope of zero.\n\n We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(0,0)$ and $(3,0),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{0-0}{3-0}=\\frac{0}{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
